SM(523,{"n":"Romans 13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 13.mp3","d":2838.2,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.52,"We'll get started here in just a moment when we do."],[24.48,"We're in St."],[25.2,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, beginning of chapter 13."],[41.44,"Because if you looked ahead, you'll see that this last portion, chapters 12 through 16, is actually much shorter than the preceding portion."],[53.12,"Even the chapters are shorter than the earlier chapters, so it's it's uh considerably shorter."],[59.12,"Um, but that's because, as we mentioned in all of St."],[63.24,"Paul's epistles, in the first portion, he's laying all the groundwork, building out all the theology, all the principles, and then in the second part, he's sort of applying those things."],[75.24,"And we saw that last time as we were going through chapter 12, uh, where he started focusing more directly on unity and love within the community and concrete ways of showing that love, uh, and sort of the application of all the work he did about their community being one in theological principle, you know, rather than divided into Jews and Gentiles or any other any other sort of division that they might be carrying over from their life in the capital of the Roman Empire."],[104.44,"the And so we're going through while there are certain themes that wrap through this latter section."],[113.44,"It's sort of not like the first second."],[115.28,"The first section, you know, he was sort of building careful arguments, and there was a lot of context, and so we had to keep kind of dropping back a little bit and tracing the argument through."],[125.92,"Uh this portion is not like that because it's that application portion."],[130.96,"Uh so we don't have to sort of redo chapter 12 in detail to get into chapter 13."],[136.32,"Although, as I mentioned, there are themes that sort of uh weave through."],[141.12,"So we just finished he was talking about contrasting sort of love and the desire for revenge, and saying that vengeance doesn't belong to us."],[149.84,"Yes It belongs to God."],[155.48,"And uh so what he's gonna what we're gonna get into here at the beginning of chapter 13 is not unrelated to that that principle, but it's gonna be sort of a further fleshing out of it in other areas as we go."],[169.8,"So unless there are any leftover questions or comments or footnotes or in notes, we'll go ahead and get started in uh chapter 13, verse one."],[183.56,"Uh let every soul be subject to the governing authorities, for there is no authority except from God, and the authorities that exist are appointed by God."],[192.92,"So for this, not just this verse, but for this whole discussion, I'm gonna go way back to when we first introduced the book of Romans many moons ago, when I made the comment that a lot of the time, Saint Paul is speaking historically, not just sort of theologically in an abstract way."],[214.4,"We tend to think about theology in an abstract way."],[218.08,"So people will look at this and say, oh, well, see, the government, the civil government, you know, comes from God and derives its authority from God."],[228.32,"And he's not saying that in an abstract principle."],[231.64,"As an abstract principle."],[233.78,"He is saying that as a historical, as a historical reality."],[241.34,"So is he talking then primarily about the existing Roman government?"],[247.4,"At this time obviously right the ro the Roman Empire itself."],[251.48,"But he's referring back to, of course, in the in the old testament the whole succession of empires."],[257.32,"You know, we see like in the book of Daniel, you know, um going through the Babylonians and the Persians and that this sort of rise and fall of empires, the Assyrians before them, uh Egypt."],[268.8,"Sort of rise and fall of empires, this is all at the hand of at the hand of God."],[274.0,"And of cour,se all in the prophets, all the oracles against different nations that had become corrupt."],[279.44,"So this is not something that's pure happenstance."],[283.04,"Well, it just so happens"],[286.34,"This is certainly not an issue where, for example, um the Roman gods are engaged in some kind of battle with the god of Israel, some kind of struggle, and you know, they represent the authority of these other beings."],[301.7,"Like they're as if they're on par with each other."],[304.34,"But it's that historically God has raised up right then the Roman Empire, you know, and at some point, if and when the Roman Empire falls from St."],[316.58,"Paul's perspective, that will be God also."],[320.02,"And this is something that we see rulers themselves, Nebuchadnezzar being a particular example of the book of Daniel, get reminded of over and over again."],[327.96,"You didn't go and conquer Judah because you're so mighty or so powerful or because your God Marduk is special."],[335.24,"You came and conquered Judah because Judah had sinned and so God used you to discipline his people."],[342.84,"And so the time will come where you Nebuchadnezzar are also going to have to answer for for your sins and wickedness."],[350.44,"So this is this is the sort of well I I'm very interested in this because um I've done a lot of work with Stuart, the Stuart period in England."],[362.6,"And of cour,se King James formulates the this theory of of uh of the I hate to use the term divine right of kings because it's been abused so much."],[372.12,"the But uh but but it's that's the going term yeah yeah that's the going term."],[382.72,"And uh you know he uses the this passage uh and uh it's sort of without with or without King James it's sort of uh basic to the ideology of European monarchies uh that uh the people don't choose the king, God does."],[407.04,"Uh God by sending a baby to a royal couple has created the king that he wants."],[418.28,"So I read from the 1990s a speech given in Canada by some Russian Orthodox person to a youth group in which he applied this and said that the only God established government is the Roman Empire."],[446.44,"And the Roman Empire, of course, went through the first Rome, the second Rome, and the third Rome."],[453.16,"And now that there is no czar, uh no Orthodox person is under any obligation to any of the existing governments."],[461.88,"s. Which is perhap an overliteralization"],[468.24,"I thought it was, shall we say, an impractical theory."],[473.12,"I was a little stunned to find it in the 1990s."],[477.2,"Well, as we're gonna see as we go on, that's the exact opposite point of the point that St."],[483.52,"Paul is trying to make here."],[485.12,"But yeah, that and that's that's the divine right of king's idea reflects a sort of different, really more Western, and I say Western only because there are parallels in Roman Catholic theology as it develops during the medieval period, in the sense that they begin to see not just the papacy, the the the wh wholeole priesthood as being given powers by God, which they then are able to exercise at their will, having had those powers bestowed upon them."],[520.36,"And that's sort of the way the divine right of kings approach works is that God's made me king, he's given me this power, and therefore now I am able to utilize these powers as I see fit."],[532.2,"But we have to take, as I said, the themes weave through, we have to take chapter 13 here in connection with chapter twelve."],[539.88,"And remember, he's St."],[541.32,"Paul has just finished talking about the fact that we all have different gifts."],[546.36,"We're all different members of the same body, and that God has given us these things, and then we have a responsibility to use them in a certain way."],[552.28,"And so I would say what St"],[558.72,"Paul is getting at here from the perspective of a ruler who's reading this is you've been given these gifts."],[565.86,"Of wealth, power, etc."],[568.18,"etc. You now have a responsibility to use these for the benefit of the Christian community in the same way that anyone else with any other gift does."],[580.1,"And perhaps more responsibility because it's a much bigger gift."],[583.08,"You know, this would be a hundred talents instead of five or two."],[587.72,"Well, I think the I think the best Western kings interpreted it that way"],[594.44,"Yeah, as a responsibility, not just as rights."],[597.68,"Yeah. So he says, Therefore, whoever resists the authority resists the ordinance of God, and those who resist will bring judgment on themselves."],[611.36,"King James's favorite."],[613.04,"Yes. So the point here being that St."],[618.48,"Paul is making is disobedience is disobedience."],[623.12,"So when when God when God calls on us to obey, he doesn't just mean obey God and ignore everyone else."],[632.34,"St. Paul everywhere, and we're going to see this as we go through more of his epistles when it comes to things like the family."],[638.66,"Honor your father and your mother still applies."],[640.02,"He doesn't sort of dissolve these authority structures"],[648.38,"He talks about how those with the authority in those structures are to exercise that authority."],[655.42,"But he doesn't dissolve the authority structures into just complete chaos."],[661.02,"And so to be disobedient to your father, to the elders in your community, to the civil authorities."],[672.72,"Disobedience is disobedience, and all of that disobedience is ultimately disobedience to God."],[678.48,"Because it reflects the idea that, well, God for this time and this place has put these people in power."],[686.12,"He will hold them responsible."],[687.72,"Remember the vengeance part."],[689.66,"He will hold them responsible for how they use that power."],[692.06,"But I think I know better."],[693.66,"Uh-huh. Yeah. And so it's always an issue."],[697.88,"Disobedience is always an issue of sort of pride."],[700.76,"Well, is this the proper response to uh Martin Luther, for example, uh rebelling against the church and in today's American society being admired for for that very fact that he uh asserted the sovereignty of the individual conscience thing."],[726.44,"Well, and and I don't think I don't think St."],[728.52,"St. Paul would later in Romans we'll talk more about the role the conscience plays in"],[728.76,"Paul. So that's coming up here"],[736.16,"We probably won't get to it tonight, but that's coming up here."],[738.96,"But but yeah, in this case, I mean obviously this does not include sin."],[743.44,"Sure. Any more than, I mean, for going to the Ten Commandments, if we go to the Decalogue, the Ten Commandments, the commandment that all of this would fall under is honor your father and your mother."],[752.57,"And then by extension, other others in authority."],[756.41,"And that commandment clearly never meant well, if your father tells you to go murder someone, you'd better go murder them."],[763.05,"No. Or if your father tells you to go steal him something, you should go steal it, or whatever the case may be."],[769.55,"Um, and so there is for another Martin Luther King, you know, Martin Luther King Jr."],[775.93,"There is a point."],[777.29,"Uh St. Paul is not here denying the idea of civil disobedience."],[781.53,"Obviously, he's going to be beheaded in Rome for refusing to obey Caesar and reject Christ and worship the pagan gods."],[792.89,"Uh so he's not meaning that."],[795.21,"Clearly, that that's that that's out of bounds."],[797.77,"But even within that, St."],[799.37,"Paul did not resist being arrested."],[802.65,"He did not try to escape."],[804.25,"Uh-huh. In fact, he told people not to try and help him escape."],[809.21,"Um, he submitted himself to the authority and ultimately went to his death when they condemned him to death and trusted God to. deal with those who had executed him"],[822.77,"That hopefully they would repent of that, the same way he repented of the people he'd executed."],[830.85,"But barring that that God would be the one who would judge them for what they did."],[837.09,"He did not take that into his own hands."],[839.71,"Even when it came down to the government coming to kill him."],[843.63,"So that's the kind of obedience he's talking about."],[847.87,"Obedience as opposed to violent resistance."],[852.77,"So Martin Luther, he'd be fine with Martin Luther King."],[857.82,"Who went to jail."],[858.88,"Who was arrested, who was put in jail, who was right."],[860.28,"He might be less content with Martin Luther the original"],[866.0,"Yes, probably. I mean, if nothing else, Martin Luther, you know, interpreted his own letters all wrong."],[877.22,"But uh, you know, there were there were wars waged before Luther died."],[881.78,"So and and this this is also not just like a theoretical thing at this time in history."],[889.06,"No. There are at this time in history plenty of Jewish groups ready to take up arms against the Romans."],[898.5,"And who would over the next few decades take up arms against the Romans and receive horrible conse bring down horrible consequences not only on themselves but upon Jerusalem and then all of Judea and all of the Jew Jewish people."],[914.78,"So this is St."],[915.82,"Paul isn't just here hypothetically saying, Okay guys, obey the speed limit."],[921.02,"Uh-huh. Or, you know, don't park illegally."],[929.2,"Be a law abiding citizen."],[930.88,"This is a real pointed."],[932.8,"Well, is it also at this time are there beginning to be Christians who might be saying, Well, the Roman government is not being nice to us, and so we should be able to do that."],[944.0,"W,ell that's that's certainly what he's trying to prevent."],[946.8,"Uh-huh. That's certainly what he's trying to prevent. is Christians being a part of any of those kind of groups or movements"],[954.84,"Because of course, there isn't a separate Christianity and Judaism at this point in history."],[960.68,"But there are these Jewish resistance groups."],[965.0,"Well, and then he's setting up the Christian martyrs who don't resist uh oppression."],[973.34,"And oppression. And so he's setting up a different."],[976.3,"He's not setting up a different model, I should say."],[978.62,"There already exists a different model."],[980.62,"He's taking more of the approach of, for example, 2nd Maccabees as opposed to 1st Maccabees."],[986.14,"U-hhuh. We could neatly see the two of them right there."],[990.38,"Uh, first Maccabees, the Judas Maccabeus and his brothers, and Judas Maccabeus means Judah the hammer, which I still think is just a great underutilized baptismal name."],[1004.9,"But uh he and his brothers, in response to the Seleucid uh Emperor's attempts to de-Judaize Judea to force them to give up the Torah, responded by rising up in revolt, throwing them off, and at least temporarily establishing an independent Judea."],[1027.62,"This is the model that most of these groups are looking at."],[1032.98,"And that's what Barkokva's gonna try."],[1035.86,"That's what these other would-be messiahs are going to try."],[1038.74,"y. The see all of it in these terms"],[1044.14,"2nd Maccabees, on the other hand, the main central portion of it is telling the story of a man named Eliezer and a woman and her seven sons, who were all executed by the Seleucid governor in Antioch for refusing to violate the Torah, refusing to eat pork, refusing to offer worship to the pagan gods, and all went to their deaths willingly."],[1072.7,"And if you read 2nd Maccabees, which a lot of people haven't, but reading, I just realized read reading 2nd Maccabees, you you'll see the pattern that you see over and over again in in our Orthodox Synexarian when we you read the lives of the martyrs, you see that pattern established there."],[1092.34,"There were Judean martyrs before before this period."],[1098.9,"Uh and in fact, there there is an icon of the Maccabean martyrs that has the eight people from the uh or nine people from the the book of Maccabees, and they're all holding crosses."],[1112.5,"Uh-huh. Just like New Covenant Martyrs, and they're listed as the Maccabean martyrs."],[1117.78,"Um, so this is sort of of a piece."],[1121.06,"So, in a way, what St."],[1123.78,"Paul is doing here is pointing to 2nd Maccabees instead of 1st Maccabees."],[1128.82,"Um, not that we don't that we don't resist evil at all."],[1136.46,"But as he said at the end of chapter 12."],[1138.64,"Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good."],[1144.0,"Not with vile."],[1144.88,"So evil. So we don't fight fire with fire."],[1148.06,"They're coming to kill us, so we kill them."],[1151.28,"Or at least go down fighting and take as many of them with us as we can."],[1154.62,"But so, okay, so we offer our lives out of love for Christ."],[1158.7,"That's how we respond."],[1160.38,"And it's ultimately that approach that changes the Roman Empire."],[1165.63,"It changes the world."],[1167.15,"As opposed to what happens to the Jewish communities in Judea. who take the violent insurrection approach"],[1176.39,"For rulers are not a terror to good works, but to evil."],[1180.23,"You want to be unafraid of the authority?"],[1182.55,"Do what is good, and you will have praise from the same."],[1191.19,"Who is going to trouble you for doing good?"],[1193.45,"So if you're living a good life, you're doing good for people, you're helping people, for the most part, other than these sporadic persecutions that break out, for the most part, you're not going to have any problems."],[1207.31,"Um, because even St."],[1209.83,"Paul's pointing to even the Roman civil law, the pagan Roman civil law. is not completely, and he pointed this out, remember, all the way back in Romans 2"],[1220.59,"That the Gentiles in Romans 1 and 2 have this basic understanding murder is wrong."],[1227.52,"Theft is wrong."],[1229.04,"You know, these the and so the basic principles of Roman law are not completely separate from God's idea of justice."],[1237.34,"The Empire wasn't just random chaos and everybody killing each other in the streets."],[1242.11,"There was a civil order."],[1243.55,"And so if you if you're doing what you're supposed to be doing as a Christian, for the most part, you're not gonna run afoul of the law."],[1253.55,"For he is God's minister to you for good, but if you do evil, be afraid. for he does not bear the sword in vain"],[1261.43,"For he is God's minister and avenger to execute wrath on him who practices evil."],[1267.03,"And so you notice he uses the word minister twice."],[1271.41,"It's not the king who is taking vengeance against evil, it's God using the king."],[1276.95,"The importance. So he is, he is, he is a minister."],[1280.31,"And what is his his responsibility to you as a Christian is what?"],[1284.39,"Is to be a minister for good."],[1286.93,"To do good for you, to help you, to protect you, to aid you in living the kind of life that Christ has called you to live."],[1294.63,"By maintaining the social order and and other things."],[1298.71,"That's his job."],[1299.99,"But if you're someone who's doing evil, then you should be afraid of him."],[1304.59,"Because God also uses human authorities to do these things."],[1308.91,"Nebuchadnezzar being an example."],[1310.83,"That doesn't mean Nebuchadnezzar was We we don't have a saint Nebuchadnezzar."],[1315.45,"There's a reason."],[1316.17,"No, there's there's iffy parts of Daniel where it sounds like he may have come around."],[1319.85,"But you know, barring our inability to judge his soul."],[1323.23,"It's not saying that Nebuchadnezzar is definitely not the Assyrians."],[1327.31,"No. Definitely not the Persians."],[1330.83,"And definitely not."],[1332.35,"Cyrus gets the good rating then."],[1334.25,"Why? Because Cyrus was a minister to them for good."],[1337.61,"Yes. Cyrus carried out that responsibility faithfully, even though he remained uh a pagan."],[1344.18,"But also God used the Assyrians to discipline and punish his people"],[1350.94,"You go through the book of Judges, he used all kinds of people."],[1353.82,"The Philistines certainly aren't good guys, but when his people needed to be disciplined for their sins, God brought the Philistines in to do it."],[1363.66,"And so this is again within that paradigm of responsibility on the part of the ruler."],[1372.62,"And that it is God who is the one to whom they're responsible and who uses the rulers and kingdoms of this world to bring about his will in the world."],[1385.34,"Therefore, you must be subject not only because of wrath, but also for conscience' sake."],[1390.98,"So he's saying not sure what the distinction."],[1393.14,"Yeah. Meaning you shouldn't just be obedient to the civil rulers out of fear of them."],[1401.39,"Uh-uh. Like, oh, I better do this, or they're going to punish me in this and that and the other way."],[1407.55,"But that we should be obedient also in a positive sense."],[1412.19,"That our obedience has a positive value."],[1416.35,"By obeying them, we're obeying God."],[1419.47,"That's the flip side of."],[1420.99,"I shouldn't drive as fast as I do, not only because out of fear of getting a ticket, but out of uh concern for my safety and those of the right."],[1432.35,"And of expressing humility in terms of, you know, well, I know I can drive 80 miles an hour down this road and be fine."],[1443.69,"I know better than the guy who put the 65 sign there."],[1448.41,"Um because of this, you also pay taxes, for they are God's ministers, attending continually to this very thing."],[1460.41,"Render therefore to all their due taxes to whom taxes are due, customs to whom customs, fear to whom fear, honor to whom honor."],[1470.01,"And that fear is more like reverence."],[1472.41,"Uh-huh. Not be afraid of people who are scary."],[1476.21,"And this is this is um what St"],[1482.09,"Paul is citing here is goes way back in the Greek philosophical tradition."],[1486.49,"It goes back to Plato's Republic."],[1488.25,"This is a basic definition of what it means to be a just person in society."],[1493.77,"In the Greco-Roman perspective, that you give to everyone what they are due."],[1499.21,"Whatever duties, whatever honors they're due, whatever respect they're due, you give it to them."],[1505.21,"And so St."],[1506.41,"Paul is here saying you be a just upright person, within society."],[1512.27,"Well, this the very words here are so close to those of Christ in the famous uh Venderance Caesar passage."],[1521.23,"Um that you know raises the question in my mind, how much of the words of Christ would St."],[1530.39,"Paul have known?"],[1532.95,"Well, we know we know he knew a fair amount."],[1536.47,"I mean, I'm sure he did, but there's at least one place where he cites that we've already seen in Romans where he cites a saying of Christ that isn't in the gospels."],[1543.27,"Uh-huh. And we know coming up in uh when we get into Corinthians that uh he's gonna say, you know, I I passed on to you what I myself perceived that Christ on the night he was betrayed took credit where he tells the whole story."],[1561.51,"So he was the recipient of a lot of this, probably more than we have. now, judging by at least that one that one quotation"],[1570.75,"He at least would have definitely known about the attitude of Christ toward the civil authorities and be trying to reflect that here."],[1580.67,"More than once in more than one way."],[1582.81,"We have that one uh very clear passage."],[1585.45,"And with without digressing too far, but any time that gets brought up, I want to comment on it, because that's one of the most misinterpreted uh passages in scripture."],[1596.17,"That's because the assumption is is the assumption is that uh Christ is saying, well, you shouldn't worry about material things."],[1603.77,"They belong to the material world."],[1605.69,"So you give material things to material rulers, and you give your heart to God."],[1613.19,"Then he's saying something like that, you know."],[1616.23,"And that's not at all."],[1618.63,"That's not at all what he's what he's saying."],[1622.15,"Um Christ, remember when he begins that, someone asks him if they should pay taxes to Caesar or not."],[1629.43,"He says, uh, uh, do you have a coin?"],[1632.47,"Give it to me."],[1633.19,"Takes the coin, holds it up."],[1635.61,"There's the image of Caesar on it."],[1637.29,"Uh huh. But the what what the question he asks is whose image is this?"],[1642.73,"And the word image there is econ, where we get icon."],[1647.29,"Whose icon is this?"],[1649.53,"Which is the language that's used in the Greek of Genesis."],[1654.01,"Uhh-hu. So uh as an image of God."],[1657.67,"It's so it's it's a twofold question."],[1661.83,"Well on one hand, yes, that's a picture of Caesar, but on the other hand, who who's Caesar?"],[1667.19,"Uh-huh. Caesar's made in the Caesar."],[1669.11,"He's in the image of God."],[1671.11,"So when he says give to Caesar what belongs to Caesar, okay, give him his money."],[1676.23,"Give to God what belongs to God."],[1678.15,"What is got what belongs to God?"],[1679.83,"Caesar. Everything including Caesar himself."],[1683.79,"That's why that's why everyone's sort of dumbfounded by his answer and doesn't have any response."],[1690.37,"Because they were wanting him to go one way or the other."],[1693.41,"They were wanting him to either say, Oh, yeah, you should pay taxes to Caesar."],[1697.53,"Or and then they'd say, Oh, look, this guy's a collaborator."],[1701.61,"Or, no, you shouldn't, and then they could say, Oh, see, look, this guy's trying to start a rebellion against the Romans."],[1707.21,"And so the usual way it's interpreted is essentially that he said, Yeah, you should just pay your taxes, which would not have dumbfounded them."],[1713.93,"It would have been falling into their trap."],[1715.93,"So, but that's what he was actually saying."],[1717.61,"That's why they're dumbfounded, because they don't know what to say to that."],[1720.37,"Because what are you gonna say?"],[1721.81,"Caesar isn't the image of God."],[1725.97,"Well. Okay. So yeah, that's that's what he's that's what that's what Christ is uh is getting at there."],[1735.41,"And so this is on a more basic level."],[1737.25,"This is on the level of whose coin is it?"],[1739.33,"Well, okay. So this is give to just give to everyone what they're due"],[1744.65,"Live a just and and upright life in the world."],[1749.83,"And even if it comes down to an unjust or evil ruler coming to take your life unnecessarily, we don't take up arms against the practically Stoicism."],[1761.99,"Very close to the world."],[1762.95,"Yes, yeah. Of course, the Stoics derive from the earlier person."],[1766.85,"And this is this is a this is a rabbitorial I'm not gonna go down, but there is a lot to be said about uh there are a couple places where uh St."],[1777.25,"Paul seems to directly quote Stoics."],[1779.49,"Uh huh. Um so he was very familiar with Stoic philosophy."],[1784.77,"And in in interacting with particularly Gentile converts his letters, he he seems to draw on some of that language and that kind of thing quite a bit in order to get things across to them."],[1797.21,"In the same way that he draws on the Hebrew scriptures to talk to Jewish members of the community."],[1806.81,"Yeah. To get them to the same point."],[1810.25,"So he continues, Owe no one anything except to love one another."],[1814.81,"For he who loves another has fulfilled the law."],[1818.01,"For the commandments you shall not commit adultery, you shall not murder, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness, you shall not covet, and if there is any other commandment, are all summed up in this saying namely, you should love your neighbor as yourself."],[1832.93,"Love does no harm to a neighbor, therefore love is the fulfillment of the law."],[1836.77,"So you can argue with that."],[1838.77,"So again, this is very close to what Christ says in terms of the in terms of the greatest commandment, but he breaks it down a little more in order to explain why."],[1847.73,"So the own no one anything is part of that, give to everyone what they're due."],[1853.97,"Uh and so the only obligation you should you should allow to remain outstanding is the obligation to love everyone."],[1861.91,"Which is always outstanding."],[1863.69,"You never finish that."],[1865.13,"Okay, I've loved everybody."],[1866.65,"Loved everybody. I'm done."],[1868.65,"Go rest on my laurels."],[1870.57,"And then he explains how the one who loves fulfills the whole law."],[1874.73,"Because he gives examples, gives some of the Ten Commandments"],[1878.45,"He says, any other commandment you want to bring up."],[1881.75,"If you love someone, you're not going to steal from them, you're not going to harm them, you're not going to commit adultery with their spouse, you're not going to, or with them."],[1890.96,"You're not going to do any of these things."],[1893.52,"No one ever did any of these things out of love."],[1896.0,"Did St. Augustine really say something like love God and love your neighbor and do as you please?"],[1904.24,"Which I've heard quoted."],[1906.0,"He said something similar."],[1907.44,"Uh-huh The do as you please was related to something we talked about last time, in terms of trying to figure out God's will about particular topics. in particular situations"],[1920.76,"So the idea was if you're loving God completely and loving your neighbor completely, then any choices you're making are not going to be sinful."],[1932.95,"And so. But that first part isn't isn't easy."],[1936.69,"I assume that you're doing that."],[1938.53,"And so, yes, if you're able to love God and your neighbor perfectly, then yeah, you could just do whatever you want because all of those."],[1947.09,"But also, it his point that he was getting at was, and this is something that still afflicts us."],[1954.59,"Is we want we want rules."],[1956.83,"We want rules."],[1957.48,"We want a specific breakdown."],[1958.92,"What am I allowed to do?"],[1959.88,"What am I not allowed to do?"],[1960.84,"So I can follow all the rules."],[1963.26,"And that's that's very much St."],[1965.82,"Paul's former Pharisaic approach."],[1969.44,"Is we need to follow all the rules and get everything broken down."],[1973.04,"I was speaking somewhere recently, and you know, what one of those questions came up, in this case regarding tattoos."],[1980.88,"And the person just really wanted me, I think they really wanted me to say it's okay, go get tattoos."],[1985.12,"Uh huh. Or if not that, they would have settled for, oh no, the Bible says you're not allowed to get tattoos."],[1990.32,"And I wouldn't actually give them either answer."],[1992.64,"Uh-huh. I tried to talk about sort of all the issues surrounding it and all the the topics, and I think it really frustrated that person."],[2000.56,"Uh I wouldn't get."],[2002.6,"But we're we're called to, and the point. St"],[2005.64,"Paul is making is when when he talks about love as a debt that we owe to everyone is that we're called to something higher than just the rules."],[2017.86,"And and we're called to something that we can never stop working on and never fully fulfill."],[2025.86,"So just because I haven't killed you or assaulted you today doesn't mean I've loved you."],[2032.76,"Just because I have it stolen from you doesn't mean I've loved you."],[2036.09,"Uh that love is over and above and beyond that."],[2039.61,"And the flip side of that is that if I'm doing everything I can to show love, I don't have to worry about the rules."],[2048.29,"I don't have to worry about the rules."],[2050.21,"Christ didn't have to worry what day he was healing someone on."],[2054.31,"The Pharisees were very worried that he might heal someone on the Sabbath."],[2059.05,"But he was acting out of love and compassion for people."],[2063.13,"So it didn't matter what day it was."],[2065.35,"Or where they were, or what ethnicity the person he was healing was."],[2070.47,"And so this is, and this is a recurring theme in St."],[2075.03,"Paul. And it's part of how he understands the law being fulfilled."],[2079.69,"Though There are countless passages about the law that basically are derived from this idea."],[2087.09,"And, you know, the way we tend to interpret, for example, when Christ in St."],[2092.17,"Matthew's Gospel says, I came not to abolish the law but fulfill it."],[2097.45,"We basically treat abolish and fulfill as synonyms."],[2101.7,"Either way, it's gone now, and I don't have to worry about it."],[2104.42,"And we'll say, oh no, no, it's not abolished, it's fulfilled."],[2107.38,"Uh-huh. But I still don't have to worry about it."],[2110.39,"That's not how St."],[2111.75,"Paul understands the law being fulfilled."],[2114.23,"St. Paul understands the love that comes through our indwelling of the by the Holy Spirit to be what fulfills the law in us."],[2127.03,"That's why in Philippians, he's gonna say, fruit of the Spirit are love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control."],[2135.27,"Uh-huh Against these things there is no law."],[2139.95,"Because it's not just avoiding the negative, but it's this positive duty to love."],[2148.03,"And when we do those positive things, which is by being empowered by the Holy Spirit, walking in the Spirit, as he said earlier in Romans, living our life with the Holy Spirit, then the law is fulfilled in us, in the sense that we don't break it or violate it."],[2167.95,"We go beyond the demands of the law, which is what Christ is referring to when he says, unless your righteousness exceeds that of the Pharisees and teachers of the law, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven, and everybody goes."],[2179.15,"Well, how is that possible?"],[2180.39,"They do all they have tassels on their garments."],[2184.47,"But that's not what that's not what the Torah was getting at in the first place."],[2189.67,"And do this, knowing the time, that now it is high time to awake out of sleep."],[2196.07,"For now our salvation is nearer than when we first believed."],[2199.99,"The night is far spent, the day is at hand."],[2202.79,"Therefore let us cast off the works of darkness, and let us put on the armor of light."],[2208.15,"So he's saying, look, we're we're on a a limited timetable here."],[2213.67,"Christ is going to return, and all of us are mortal."],[2220.12,"So we have this duty, this debt, this obligation to love."],[2224.44,"Not put it off"],[2225.52,"We need to actually do it."],[2229.6,"Not just theoretically, not just sit and say, Oh, that's good theology."],[2234.0,"Yes, that's true."],[2235.12,"If one is loving, then one is going to keep all of the commandments, you know."],[2241.04,"Um this is this is uh he's already dealt with this back in in chapter two, but there was actually at this point of time within Second Temple Judaism, uh this whole discussion about hearing the law versus doing it."],[2256.32,"Whether the people who are blessed are the ones who do the law, or whether people are blessed by having received it, by hearing it."],[2263.3,"So the Jewish people received the law."],[2265.62,"Does that alone make them right the chosen people?"],[2269.3,"Or is it only the ones who keep it and keep it perfectly"],[2274.78,"And what about people who aren't then Jews who keep it perfectly?"],[2278.06,"That's a lot of what St."],[2279.02,"Paul is dealing with in Romans chapter two."],[2281.42,"Talked about people being a law to themselves, you know."],[2284.3,"And so St."],[2285.1,"Paul is very much here now, redirecting us too."],[2287.58,"Yeah, and we're not don't just hear this."],[2289.88,"We need to do this."],[2291.96,"And doing this means because number one, time is short and you have this obligation, uh, but that this actually means casting off the works of darkness and putting on the armor of light, meaning the armor image, uh, because you know, it's not casting off the sword and spear of darkness."],[2313.88,"There's a sort of change of metaphor there"],[2318.13,"Because he's he's the whole metaphor he's using is one of waking up."],[2322.85,"Uh-huh. It's one of vigilance and of being on guard."],[2325.73,"So you're waking up and preparing for preparing for battle."],[2330.17,"Um as opposed to the works of dark whatever you may have been doing at night under the cover of darkness."],[2336.33,"Uh-huh. The dawn is coming."],[2338.57,"You need to wake up and be vigilant."],[2343.21,"It says, Let us walk properly as in the day, not in revelry and drunkenness, not in lewdness and lust, not in strife and envy."],[2352.81,"Is this where it's supposed to say chambering in one-ness?"],[2355.85,"Yes. Too bad it does."],[2357.37,"es. In the old King James, y, leave off chambering"],[2364.13,"I love that."],[2365.65,"But yes, that's the lewdness and lust."],[2369.17,"That's sometimes translated sexual immorality and sensuality, uh, depending on your translation."],[2376.53,"But you get the idea."],[2377.97,"These are all things that go on at night."],[2380.53,"These are all things that go on at night at your typical Roman dinner party."],[2384.53,"Uh-huh. Including the strife and envy."],[2387.39,"That's true. And the revelry and drunkenness and the sexual immorality."],[2392.11,"I've been to parties like that."],[2394.11,"Usually after prong."],[2395.47,"Yeah. But put on the Lord Jesus Christ and make no provision for the flesh to fulfill its lusts."],[2401.55,"He's actually repeating."],[2402.19,"He actually said that earlier"],[2404.95,"But he's he's calling us back to it."],[2407.21,"We have this obligation of love that we owe to everyone."],[2411.85,"We have no obligation to our flesh to fulfill its desires."],[2419.45,"It wants us to feel like we do."],[2422.41,"It's pretty good."],[2424.01,"So but but we do not."],[2426.41,"And this is this is actually what St."],[2428.81,"Paul is saying here is very important uh in our modern day because it has uh a I don't know if I want to say new or renewed relevance uh thanks to one Sigmund Freud."],[2442.57,"Oh, yes. Uh which modern psychanal psychoanalysis and those following it, of course, the the problem or the source of all of our problems is the repression of our natural desires."],[2459.1,"If someone has a desire, that desire is not able to be fulfilled in some way, that is a problem that will cause problems for them and destroy their life."],[2469.1,"This is getting off, I yeah."],[2471.34,"But Freud we should sublimate it into positive ways."],[2476.92,"Find positive ways."],[2478.45,"But don't just repress it."],[2480.21,"But in our society, that whole positive element has increasingly been stripped away."],[2487.47,"And so much of the argumentation in our society around sexuality, for example, even when these discussions come up in the church, are centered around this idea."],[2500.22,"Well, if we say that if we say that homosexuality, or if we say that polyamory now, or if we say that any of these things aren't allowed, if we just forbid forbid them, then these people who have these desires were doing some sort of violence to them by making them spend a life unfulfilled."],[2524.24,"Because, you know, they they have, you know, the this uh this desire."],[2530.56,"But the whole presupposition there is flawed meant to be flawed."],[2533.52,"the Is that that the fulfillment of and this flies in face of not just what St."],[2541.22,"Paul is saying here, but the entire structure of morality for all of human history, even among the pagans."],[2549.0,"Was that morality was specifically not fulfilling all of your desires."],[2555.64,"That's what separated a moral and immoral person."],[2558.04,"I don't know any moral system that isn't based on the idea of self-discipline, uh of not following your whims."],[2567.54,"Right right and and we're stoicism being a key example."],[2570.86,"And we've tried we've turned that on its head."],[2573.42,"And so this is very, this is very pertinent now of of what do we owe we don't owe ourselves the fulfillment our desires uh huh we owe God and our neighbor love that's the debt we need to spend the rest of our life repaying not trying to satisfy the flesh and and so that's that's broader than sexuality uh it means that uh desire for wealth desire for for wealth or uh the world's greatest collection of whatever right right uh you know constant entertainment, yeah, constant entertainment, all of these things uh are included in that."],[2621.11,"Yeah. ah Ye, there were more limited entertainment options."],[2625.75,"St. Paul St."],[2626.87,"Paul ran through them."],[2628.09,"Drunkenness, revelry, yeah, chambering and strife and envy, you know, gossiping and yeah and intrigue."],[2638.01,"Um now taken to new levels via social media."],[2640.99,"But yes, and so that's he's he's asking us to shift our thinking."],[2646.11,"Because our natural thinking is to think on that animal level."],[2650.91,"Yeah. You know, my dogs have desires."],[2655.89,"They want food, they want water, they want attention, and basically everything they do is to get one of those things."],[2663.86,"Uh or to sleep."],[2669.5,"But it's very easy for us as human beings to sink to that level."],[2674.3,"But even speaking of domestic pets, even my cats have to learn a little discipline."],[2688.62,"Yeah. Training cats is, I imagine, difficult."],[2692.38,"With dogs, it's you give them one of those things that they want in return for the behavior that you want."],[2698.7,"It doesn't work that way."],[2699.82,"Yeah. So, you know, you will get food, a treat, if you do this."],[2705.02,"And so they will do that, not out of love for me, or some kind of selflessness, but to get to satisfy that desire."],[2715.14,"And so yeah, it's very easy for humans to sink to that level of just, you know, and treating other people, treating relationships, treating situations as how can I use this to satisfy some desire of mine."],[2731.38,"Well, you know, now we're actually teaching people this because it's these are not current, but they're not that long ago that uh there were posters and things uh saying uh if it feels good, do it."],[2745.86,"Yeah. Uh he who dies with the most toys wins."],[2749.3,"Uh it's it's a sort of moral system that is the negation of any previous moral system."],[2755.06,"Yeah, yeah It's the idea that we're here to live the good life instead of live a good life."],[2763.79,"Yes. And so St."],[2766.27,"Paul strikes directly at that."],[2767.71,"Because even then, even though your average Roman would have agreed in principle, uh huh, you don't have to read very much in Roman literature to understand that there was a constant crisis of morals."],[2779.23,"Oh, yeah. And every every major philosophical writer and even emperors and government officials are constantly writing about the need for moral reform."],[2790.99,"Because even though they at least tacitly accepted that yes, we need to exercise self-control and self-restraint, they were not good at doing it."],[2798.83,"Well, that's uh that's pretty much the human condition"],[2799.07,"Yeah. Yeah. And that's that's why St"],[2805.29,"Paul here is ultimately saying that it requires the Holy Spirit dwelling within us and walking in the Spirit for us to do that."],[2815.74,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2823.18,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2828.54,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":24.48,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13 - Application section recap","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chapters <b>12-16</b> = much <b>shorter</b> than the first portion</li><li class=\"d1\">shorter even chapter by chapter</li><li class=\"d0\">Same pattern as all Paul's epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">first part: <b>groundwork</b>, theology, principles</li><li class=\"d1\">second part: <b>applying</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 12: <b>unity and love</b> in the community, concrete ways to show it</li><li class=\"d1\">community <b>one in principle</b>, not divided into Jews and Gentiles or any other division</li><li class=\"d0\">No need to redo ch. 12 to get into 13 - not like the careful <b>argument-tracing</b> of part one</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>themes weave through</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Just finished: <b>love vs. desire for revenge</b> - vengeance belongs to <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 13 = further fleshing out of that principle in other areas</li></ul>"},{"t":183.56,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:1-2 - Governing authorities","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:1</b>: \"let every soul be subject to the governing authorities... no authority except from God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to the <b>intro to Romans</b>: Paul often speaking <b>historically</b>, not abstractly</li><li class=\"d1\">we think of theology <b>abstractly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people read: civil government derives authority from God as <b>abstract principle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says it as a <b>historical reality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Primarily the <b>existing Roman government</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but looks back to the OT <b>succession of empires</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Daniel</b>: Babylonians, Persians; Assyrians before them; Egypt</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>rise and fall of empires</b> = at the <b>hand of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all the <b>prophets' oracles against corrupt nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>happenstance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not Roman gods battling the God of Israel, <b>on par</b> with each other</li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>raised up</b> Rome - if Rome falls, from Paul's view <b>that will be God too</b></li></ul>"},{"t":320.02,"lv":3,"h":"Nebuchadnezzar reminded of this","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rulers themselves <b>reminded over and over</b> - <b>Nebuchadnezzar</b> the example in Daniel</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't conquer Judah because you're <b>mighty</b>, or because <b>Marduk</b> is special</li><li class=\"d1\">conquered because <b>Judah had sinned</b> - God used you to <b>discipline his people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">your time will come: <b>answer for your own sins and wickedness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":350.44,"lv":3,"h":"Divine right of kings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q (a listener who works on the <b>Stuart period</b>): <b>King James</b> and the <b>divine right of kings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he hates the term - \"abused so much\" - but <b>it's the going term</b></li><li class=\"d1\">James uses this passage</li><li class=\"d1\">basic to ideology of <b>European monarchies</b>: people don't choose the king, <b>God does</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"by sending a baby to a royal couple\" God <b>created the king he wants</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: 1990s speech to a youth group in Canada by a Russian Orthodox speaker</li><li class=\"d1\">only God-established government = <b>Roman Empire</b>: first, second, <b>third Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no czar now → no Orthodox person <b>obliged to any existing government</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"impractical theory\" - <b>exact opposite</b> of Paul's point here</li><li class=\"d0\">Divine right reflects a more <b>Western</b> idea</li><li class=\"d1\">parallels in <b>Roman Catholic</b> theology in the <b>medieval period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just papacy, whole <b>priesthood</b> given powers by God to exercise</li><li class=\"d1\">king: \"God's made me king, given me this power, so I use it <b>as I see fit</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":532.2,"lv":3,"h":"Ruler's gifts and responsibility","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>ch. 13 with ch. 12</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul just said: <b>different gifts</b>, members of the same body, <b>responsibility</b> to use them</li><li class=\"d0\">To a <b>ruler</b> reading this: you've been given gifts - <b>wealth, power</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>responsibility</b> to use them for the <b>Christian community's benefit</b>, like anyone else</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>more</b> responsibility - bigger gift: \"a <b>hundred talents</b> instead of five or two\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Best Western kings read it that way: <b>responsibility, not just rights</b></li></ul>"},{"t":597.68,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:2 - Disobedience is disobedience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:2</b>: whoever resists the authority <b>resists the ordinance of God</b> - \"King James's favorite\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>disobedience is disobedience</b></li><li class=\"d1\">when God calls us to obey, not just \"obey God and <b>ignore everyone else</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in Paul on the <b>family</b>: <b>honor your father and mother</b> still applies</li><li class=\"d1\">he doesn't <b>dissolve authority structures</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he tells those <b>with</b> authority how to exercise it</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>complete chaos</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Disobedience to <b>father, community elders, civil authorities</b> = ultimately to <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God put these people in power <b>for this time and place</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>He</b> will hold them responsible - \"remember the <b>vengeance</b> part\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"but I think I know better\" → always an issue of <b>pride</b></li></ul>"},{"t":700.76,"lv":2,"h":"Q: Luther, conscience, civil disobedience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: proper response to <b>Martin Luther</b> - rebelling, admired for asserting <b>individual conscience</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">role of <b>conscience</b> comes <b>later in Romans</b> - not tonight</li><li class=\"d0\">Obviously <b>does not include sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">under the Decalogue: <b>honor your father and mother</b>, extended to others in authority</li><li class=\"d1\">never meant: father says <b>murder</b> or <b>steal</b>, you do it</li><li class=\"d0\">Not denying <b>civil disobedience</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>beheaded in Rome</b> for refusing to obey Caesar, reject Christ, worship pagan gods</li><li class=\"d0\">But Paul <b>didn't resist arrest</b>, didn't escape, told people <b>not to help him escape</b></li><li class=\"d1\">submitted, went to his death, <b>trusted God</b> to deal with executioners</li><li class=\"d1\">hoped they'd <b>repent</b> as he repented of those he executed; else <b>God judges</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't take it into <b>his own hands</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Obedience <b>as opposed to violent resistance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so fine with <b>Martin Luther King</b> (went to jail); less so with Luther - <b>wars before Luther died</b></li></ul>"},{"t":881.78,"lv":3,"h":"Not hypothetical - Jewish revolt groups","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not a theoretical issue in that history</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jewish groups</b> ready to take up arms against Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">over next decades did → <b>horrible consequences</b> on themselves, <b>Jerusalem</b>, all <b>Judea</b>, the Jewish people</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul isn't saying \"obey the <b>speed limit</b>, don't <b>park illegally</b>\" - this is <b>pointed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Christians saying \"Rome isn't nice to us, so we can too\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">that's what he's <b>trying to prevent</b>: Christians joining such movements</li><li class=\"d1\">no separate <b>Christianity and Judaism</b> yet - but <b>resistance groups</b> exist</li><li class=\"d0\">Sets up <b>martyrs</b> who don't resist - model already exists</li></ul>"},{"t":980.62,"lv":3,"h":"2 Maccabees vs. 1 Maccabees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul takes <b>2 Maccabees</b>, not <b>1 Maccabees</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Maccabees</b>: <b>Judas Maccabeus</b> (\"Judah the hammer\" - \"underutilized baptismal name\") and brothers</li><li class=\"d1\">Seleucid emperor tried to <b>de-Judaize Judea</b>, force giving up <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they <b>revolted</b>, threw them off, temporarily <b>independent Judea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the model most groups looked to; <b>Bar Kokhba</b>, other would-be messiahs try it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Maccabees</b>: <b>Eleazar</b>, a mother and her <b>seven sons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">executed by Seleucid governor in <b>Antioch</b> for refusing <b>pork</b>, <b>pagan worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all went to death <b>willingly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same pattern as <b>lives of the martyrs</b> in the Orthodox <b>Synaxarion</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Icon of the <b>Maccabean martyrs</b> - eight or nine, all <b>holding crosses</b>, like New Covenant martyrs</li><li class=\"d0\">Not that we don't resist evil: <b>12:21</b> - overcome evil <b>with good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't <b>fight fire with fire</b> or go down fighting</li><li class=\"d1\">we <b>offer our lives out of love for Christ</b> → that <b>changes the Roman Empire</b>, the world</li><li class=\"d1\">vs. what happens to Judean Jews who took <b>violent insurrection</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1176.39,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:3-4 - Rulers as God's ministers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:3</b>: rulers not a terror to <b>good works</b>, but to evil</li><li class=\"d1\">do good → <b>praise</b> from same; \"who is going to trouble you for doing good?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">apart from <b>sporadic persecutions</b>, mostly no problems</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>pagan Roman civil law</b> not wholly separate from God's justice</li><li class=\"d1\">back in <b>Romans 1-2</b>: Gentiles know <b>murder, theft wrong</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Empire wasn't <b>random chaos</b>; there was <b>civil order</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:4</b>: <b>minister</b> of God for good; <b>bears the sword</b> not in vain; <b>avenger</b> to execute wrath</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>minister used twice</b> - not the king taking vengeance, <b>God using the king</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ruler's job to you: <b>minister for good</b> - protect, aid, <b>maintain social order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if you do evil, <b>be afraid</b> - God also uses human authorities for that</li></ul>"},{"t":1308.91,"lv":3,"h":"Nebuchadnezzar, Cyrus, Philistines","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Nebuchadnezzar</b> = God's instrument, not a good guy - <b>no St. Nebuchadnezzar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">iffy parts of Daniel where he may have come around - <b>can't judge his soul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not the <b>Assyrians</b>, not the <b>Persians</b> generally</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cyrus</b> gets the good rating: <b>minister for good</b>, faithful though a <b>pagan</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Assyrians <b>disciplined and punished</b> his people</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Judges</b>: Philistines not good guys, but God brought them to <b>discipline</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All within a paradigm of <b>ruler's responsibility</b> to <b>God</b>, who uses <b>kingdoms of this world</b> for his will</li></ul>"},{"t":1385.34,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:5 - For conscience' sake","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:5</b>: subject not only because of <b>wrath</b> but for <b>conscience' sake</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not only obedient out of <b>fear</b> - \"I'll be punished\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Obedience has <b>positive value</b>: by obeying them, <b>we obey God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just fear of a <b>ticket</b> but concern for <b>safety</b> of others</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>humility</b>: \"I can drive <b>80</b> on this road... I know better than the guy who put the <b>65 sign</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1448.41,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:6-7 - Taxes and what is due","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:6</b>: pay <b>taxes</b> - God's ministers attending continually to this</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:7</b>: render to all their due - taxes, <b>customs</b>, <b>fear</b>, <b>honor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fear = <b>reverence</b>, not being afraid of scary people</li><li class=\"d0\">Goes back to <b>Greek philosophy</b>, <b>Plato's Republic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basic definition of a <b>just person</b>: give everyone <b>what they're due</b> - duties, honors, respect</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: be a <b>just, upright person</b> in society</li></ul>"},{"t":1512.27,"lv":2,"h":"Christ's words - the coin of Caesar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: so close to Christ's <b>\"render to Caesar\"</b> - how much of Christ's words did Paul know?</li><li class=\"d1\">a fair amount - cites a saying of Christ <b>not in the gospels</b> (already in Romans)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Corinthians</b>: \"I passed on what I received\" - <b>night he was betrayed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">likely knew <b>more than we have</b>; surely knew Christ's <b>attitude to civil authorities</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Most misinterpreted</b> passage in Scripture</li><li class=\"d1\">usual reading: material things to material rulers, <b>heart to God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not at all</b> what he says</li><li class=\"d0\">Someone asks: pay taxes to Caesar or not? \"<b>Do you have a coin?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">his question: <b>whose image?</b> = <i>eikon</i> - <b>icon</b>, the Genesis language</li><li class=\"d1\">Caesar is made <b>in the image of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"give to God what belongs to God\" = <b>everything, including Caesar himself</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why they're <b>dumbfounded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wanted <b>collaborator</b> (pay) or <b>rebel</b> (don't pay)</li><li class=\"d1\">the usual reading = \"just pay\" would have <b>fallen into their trap</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's level here: <b>whose coin is it</b>? give everyone <b>what they're due</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Unjust ruler takes your life → <b>we don't take up arms</b> - \"practically <b>Stoicism</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>quotes Stoics</b> in places; <b>familiar</b> with Stoic philosophy</li><li class=\"d1\">draws on it for <b>Gentile converts</b>, as on Hebrew Scriptures for Jews</li></ul>"},{"t":1810.25,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:8-10 - Owe nothing but love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:8</b>: <b>owe no one anything except to love</b>; he who loves <b>fulfilled the law</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:9</b>: adultery, murder, stealing, false witness, coveting - <b>summed up</b>: love your neighbor as yourself</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:10</b>: love does no harm → <b>love is the fulfillment of the law</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Close to Christ's <b>greatest commandment</b>, but breaks it down to explain <b>why</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Owe no one\" ties to <b>give everyone what's due</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the one debt that stays outstanding = <b>love</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>never finished</b>: \"I've loved everybody, I'm done, rest on my laurels\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If you love someone: won't <b>steal, harm, commit adultery</b> - nobody did these <b>out of love</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1896.0,"lv":2,"h":"Augustine and rules vs. love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Augustine</b> - \"love God and love your neighbor and <b>do as you please</b>\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">said something similar - tied to last time: finding <b>God's will</b> in particular situations</li><li class=\"d1\">love God and neighbor <b>completely</b> → choices <b>won't be sinful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but \"that first part isn't easy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>we want rules</b> - what am I allowed / not allowed</li><li class=\"d1\">very much Paul's <b>former Pharisaic approach</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>tattoos</b> question when speaking recently</li><li class=\"d1\">person wanted \"go get tattoos\" or \"Bible says no\" - <b>neither answer</b> given; talked about the <b>surrounding issues</b>; <b>frustrated</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Love as a debt = called to <b>something higher than the rules</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>never stop working on</b> it, never fully fulfill</li><li class=\"d1\">not having killed or stolen from you today ≠ <b>I've loved you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if I'm doing all I can to show love, <b>don't worry about the rules</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ</b> didn't worry what <b>day</b> he healed on, or the person's <b>ethnicity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2070.47,"lv":3,"h":"Law fulfilled by the Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recurring theme in Paul: <b>how the law is fulfilled</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Matthew: \"I came <b>not to abolish</b> but to <b>fulfill</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we treat them as synonyms: \"it's gone either way\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not how Paul understands it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Love through the indwelling Holy Spirit</b> fulfills the law <b>in us</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fruit of the Spirit</b>: love, joy, peace... \"against these things there is <b>no law</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just avoiding negative but <b>positive duty to love</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Walking in the Spirit</b> (earlier in Romans) → we <b>go beyond</b> the law's demands</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: unless your righteousness <b>exceeds the Pharisees'</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they have <b>tassels</b> on their garments\" - that's <b>not what the Torah was getting at</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2189.67,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:11-12 - Time to awake","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:11-12</b>: <b>high time to awake out of sleep</b>; salvation nearer; <b>night far spent, day at hand</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cast off <b>works of darkness</b>, put on <b>armor of light</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Limited timetable</b>: Christ will <b>return</b>, all of us are <b>mortal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>duty, debt</b> to love - <b>don't put it off</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"good theology\" - <b>actually do it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Already dealt with in <b>ch. 2</b>: <b>hearing vs. doing the law</b> in <b>Second Temple Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">are the blessed those who <b>received</b> it, or those who <b>keep</b> it perfectly?</li><li class=\"d1\">what of non-Jews who keep it? → <b>law to themselves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>redirects</b>: don't just hear - <b>do</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Change of metaphor</b>: not \"sword and spear of darkness\" but <b>armor of light</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whole image = <b>waking up</b>, <b>vigilance</b>, being on guard, preparing for battle</li><li class=\"d1\">vs. what you did <b>at night</b> under cover of darkness; <b>dawn is coming</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2343.21,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 13:13-14 - Walk as in the day","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:13</b>: walk <b>properly as in the day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>revelry and drunkenness</b>, <b>lewdness and lust</b>, <b>strife and envy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old KJV: \"<b>chambering and wantonness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">lewdness/lust = <b>sexual immorality and sensuality</b> in other translations</li><li class=\"d0\">All the things at a <b>typical Roman dinner party</b> at night - including <b>strife and envy</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:14</b>: <b>put on the Lord Jesus Christ</b>; <b>no provision for the flesh</b> to fulfill its lusts</li><li class=\"d1\">repeats an earlier point</li><li class=\"d1\">we owe <b>love to everyone</b>; we owe the <b>flesh nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the flesh \"wants us to feel like we do\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2426.41,"lv":2,"h":"Freud and fulfilling desires","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">New relevance \"thanks to one <b>Sigmund Freud</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">modern <b>psychoanalysis</b>: source of problems = <b>repression of natural desires</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unfulfilled desire <b>destroys</b> a life</li><li class=\"d1\">Freud: <b>sublimate</b> into positive ways - not repress</li><li class=\"d0\">Society has <b>stripped the positive</b> element</li><li class=\"d1\">argument around <b>sexuality</b>, even in the church: forbidding = <b>violence</b> against people, a life <b>unfulfilled</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whole presupposition <b>flawed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Against Paul <b>and the whole structure of morality in all human history</b>, even <b>pagans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">morality = <b>not fulfilling all your desires</b>; what separated moral from immoral</li><li class=\"d1\">no moral system not based on <b>self-discipline</b> - <b>Stoicism</b> a key example; we've <b>turned it on its head</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We <b>don't owe ourselves</b> fulfillment of desires</li><li class=\"d1\">we owe <b>God and neighbor love</b> - the debt to repay <b>the rest of our life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Broader than sexuality: desire for <b>wealth</b>, <b>constant entertainment</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2640.99,"lv":3,"h":"Thinking on the animal level","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul asks us to <b>shift our thinking</b> - natural thinking is <b>animal level</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His <b>dogs</b>: want food, water, attention; <b>everything they do</b> to get one</li><li class=\"d1\">train with treats: they behave to <b>satisfy a desire</b>, not out of love for me</li><li class=\"d0\">Humans <b>sink to that level</b>: treating <b>people, relationships, situations</b> as \"how can I use this to satisfy a desire\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>cats</b> have to learn some discipline</li></ul>"},{"t":2731.38,"lv":3,"h":"\"If it feels good, do it\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Posters: \"<b>if it feels good, do it</b>\"; \"<b>he who dies with the most toys wins</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">a moral system that is the <b>negation of any previous moral system</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here to live <b>the</b> good life instead of live <b>a</b> good life</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>strikes directly</b> at that</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>average Romans</b> agreed in principle</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>constant crisis of morals</b> in Roman literature</li><li class=\"d1\">philosophers, <b>emperors</b>, officials all writing about need for <b>moral reform</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accepted self-restraint, <b>weren't good at it</b> - \"the human condition\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's answer: requires the <b>Holy Spirit dwelling within us</b>, <b>walking in the Spirit</b></li></ul>"}]});
